From: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drm/meson_hdmi: restore at an HPD rise under the locks, and
 keep the first saved state

The HPD-rise restore called drm_mode_config_helper_resume(), whose
drm_atomic_helper_resume() runs drm_mode_config_reset() before it takes
any modeset lock. meson_crtc_reset() frees the live CRTC state (and the
plane and connector resets theirs) while a commit that has already
swapped in its state is running. With a userspace mode set in flight at
the rise, commit_hw_done WARNs on the stale event (drm_atomic_helper.c
2383), or, when the zeroed replacement reuses the freed object, the
commit loses its commit pointer and commit_cleanup_done deletes the
previous commit a second time: list_del corruption and a panic
(CONFIG_BUG_ON_DATA_CORRUPTION). Both were seen under a fast fake_plug
pattern, the panic on G12B.

That reset is for driver load and system resume, where the hardware
state was lost. At an HPD rise nothing was lost. Restore with
drm_atomic_helper_commit_duplicated_state() under
DRM_MODESET_LOCK_ALL, without the reset; it points each saved state's
old state at the live one, so the commit is an ordinary transition from
what is really lit. The rest is drm_mode_config_helper_resume()'s
order: clear suspend_state, fbdev un-suspend, poll enable. The system
resume path (am_meson_drm_pm_resume) is not changed.

The reset also made every restore a full modeset (the CRTC started
disabled), so the returning sink's link was always set up again (SCDC,
HDCP). Without it, a commit that lit the same mode after the plug-out
would leave the restore a plane update. Clear hdmitx_on at the rise,
under connection_mutex as the plug-out does, so the next modeset
(the restore under Y, userspace's under N) sets the link up again.
Like the branch it relies on (meson_hdmitx_atomic_check), this runs
only when android_path is 0; CoreELEC runs with android_path 1
(hdcp_ctl_lvl 0), where a same-state restore is not a modeset.

A repeated plug-out (fake_plug, the probe's fire and PM resume have no
repeat check; the IRQ paths ignore one) saved the already dark state
over the lit one, so the rise restored nothing. Keep the first saved
state and skip the second suspend. When something committed between
the two plug-outs, the rise now restores the older, first state. This
replaces the put 0023 added for the same case. Under hpd_restore=N the
rise still drops the state.

---
diff --git a/drivers/drm/meson_hdmi.c b/drivers/drm/meson_hdmi.c
--- a/drivers/drm/meson_hdmi.c
+++ b/drivers/drm/meson_hdmi.c
@@ -2613,6 +2613,38 @@
 	spin_unlock(&hpd_cb_lock);
 }
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_CEC_NOTIFIER
+/*
+ * The HPD-rise restore: drm_mode_config_helper_resume() without the
+ * drm_mode_config_reset() its drm_atomic_helper_resume() runs before taking
+ * any lock. That reset is for driver load and system resume, where the
+ * hardware state was lost. At an HPD rise nothing was lost, and the reset
+ * freed the live CRTC, plane and connector states under a commit already in
+ * flight (a use-after-free: commit_hw_done WARNs, or a double list_del in
+ * commit_cleanup_done and a panic). commit_duplicated_state points each
+ * saved state's old state at the live one, so this is an ordinary commit
+ * from what is really lit, made under every modeset lock.
+ */
+static void meson_hdmitx_hpd_resume(struct drm_device *drm)
+{
+	struct drm_atomic_state *state = drm->mode_config.suspend_state;
+	struct drm_modeset_acquire_ctx ctx;
+	int err;
+
+	DRM_MODESET_LOCK_ALL_BEGIN(drm, ctx, 0, err);
+	err = drm_atomic_helper_commit_duplicated_state(state, &ctx);
+	DRM_MODESET_LOCK_ALL_END(drm, ctx, err);
+	drm_atomic_state_put(state);
+	if (err)
+		DRM_ERROR("hpd resume failed (%d)\n", err);
+	drm->mode_config.suspend_state = NULL;
+
+	drm_fb_helper_set_suspend_unlocked(drm->fb_helper, 0);
+	if (drm->mode_config.poll_enabled)
+		drm_kms_helper_poll_enable(drm);
+}
+#endif
+
 static void meson_hdmitx_hpd_cb(void *data)
 {
 	struct am_hdmi_tx *am_hdmi = (struct am_hdmi_tx *)data;
@@ -2656,8 +2688,21 @@
 		cec_notifier_set_phys_addr_from_edid(am_hdmi->cec_notifier,
 						     pedid);
 		if (drm->mode_config.suspend_state) {
+			/*
+			 * The sink came back: its link must be set up again
+			 * (SCDC, HDCP); the reset forced that by making every
+			 * restore start from a disabled CRTC. hdmitx_on 0
+			 * forces the modeset, as after the plug-out above,
+			 * but like that branch only off android_path: with
+			 * android_path 1 a same-state restore commits nothing.
+			 */
+			if (!am_hdmi->android_path) {
+				drm_modeset_lock(mode_lock, NULL);
+				am_hdmi_info.hdmitx_on = 0;
+				drm_modeset_unlock(mode_lock);
+			}
 			if (restore) {
-				drm_mode_config_helper_resume(drm);
+				meson_hdmitx_hpd_resume(drm);
 			} else {
 				/*
 				 * hpd_restore=N: drop the saved state and do the
@@ -2678,15 +2723,16 @@
 		cec_notifier_set_phys_addr(am_hdmi->cec_notifier,
 					   CEC_PHYS_ADDR_INVALID);
 		/*
-		 * A repeated plug-out (fake_plug has no repeat check) would
-		 * overwrite and leak the state an earlier one saved: put it,
-		 * but only once the new suspend has stored its own.
+		 * A repeated plug-out (fake_plug, the probe's fire and PM resume
+		 * have no repeat check; the IRQ paths ignore one) keeps the
+		 * first saved state: a second suspend would save the already
+		 * dark state over the lit one, and the rise would restore
+		 * nothing.
 		 */
-		held = drm->mode_config.suspend_state;
-		if (!drm_mode_config_helper_suspend(drm) && held) {
-			drm_atomic_state_put(held);
-			DRM_INFO("hpd cb: repeated plug-out, earlier saved state put\n");
-		}
+		if (drm->mode_config.suspend_state)
+			DRM_INFO("hpd cb: repeated plug-out, first saved state kept\n");
+		else
+			drm_mode_config_helper_suspend(drm);
 	}
 #endif
 
